---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="Methodology — effiq"
  description="How effiq ranks reasoning variants: intelligence floor, Efficiency Score weights, sources, profiles, approximations, and data freshness."
>
  <header class="sticky top-0 z-50 border-b border-border/80 bg-background/85 backdrop-blur-md">
    <div class="mx-auto flex max-w-3xl items-center gap-3 px-4 py-3">
      <a href="/" class="flex items-center gap-2.5 no-underline">
        <span
          class="flex size-8 items-center justify-center rounded-lg bg-primary text-sm font-bold tracking-tight text-primary-foreground shadow-sm"
          aria-hidden="true"
        >
          eq
        </span>
        <span class="flex flex-col leading-none">
          <span class="text-lg font-bold tracking-tight text-foreground">effiq</span>
          <span class="text-[10px] font-medium uppercase tracking-[0.14em] text-muted-foreground">
            efficiency × iq
          </span>
        </span>
      </a>
      <span class="ml-auto text-xs font-medium text-muted-foreground">Methodology</span>
    </div>
  </header>

  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-10 prose prose-neutral dark:prose-invert prose-headings:scroll-mt-24 prose-table:text-sm">
    <p><a href="/" class="text-sm no-underline text-primary hover:underline">Back to explorer</a></p>
    <h1>Methodology</h1>

    <p>
      <strong>effiq</strong> ranks LLM <strong>reasoning variants</strong> by capability per task dollar.
      A reasoning variant is one model at one effort level, such as <em>medium</em> or <em>high</em>.
    </p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        The product question is not "which model is smartest."
        The product question is which variant delivers enough capability for the least real or estimated task dollar.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>
      The default <strong>intelligence floor</strong> keeps the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index at
      <strong>40</strong> or more.
      You can change that floor and the metric weights in the
      <a href="/">model explorer</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Default ranking settings</h2>
    <p>These values are the explorer defaults until you change them.</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Setting</th>
          <th scope="col">Default</th>
          <th scope="col">Meaning</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Intelligence floor</td>
          <td><strong>40</strong></td>
          <td>Variants below this Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index leave the table.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Efficiency Score weights</td>
          <td>35 / 15 / 10 / 30 / 5 / 5</td>
          <td>Intelligence, coding, agentic, task cost, latency, throughput.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Conservative bounds</td>
          <td>On</td>
          <td>Weak estimates use a worse bound: higher cost, lower capability.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Approximations</td>
          <td>On</td>
          <td>If confidence is high enough, rows with interpolated or family estimates stay in the table.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <p>effiq merges public provider and benchmark evidence into one matrix.</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Source</th>
          <th scope="col">What it supplies</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <a href="https://artificialanalysis.ai" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>Artificial Analysis</strong></a>
          </td>
          <td>
            Intelligence, coding, and agentic indexes. Measured cost per task. Throughput and time to first token.
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <a href="https://openrouter.ai" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>OpenRouter</strong></a>
          </td>
          <td>Live provider prices, context length, and endpoint latency and throughput percentiles.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>
            <a href="https://cursor.com/docs/models-and-pricing" rel="noopener noreferrer"><strong>Cursor</strong></a>
          </td>
          <td>Subscription-pool variants, effort and thinking axes, and published token prices.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      We do not run WhatLLM and LLM Stats adapters until robots and terms checks pass.
      Their derived scores never override Artificial Analysis or official provider prices.
    </p>

    <h2>Efficiency Score</h2>
    <p>
      The <strong>Efficiency Score</strong> is the primary ranking number in the explorer.
      Only variants that pass the intelligence floor enter the score.
    </p>
    <p>The score follows these steps:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Percentile-rank intelligence, coding, agentic, and throughput among the gated set.</li>
      <li>Log-normalize task cost and latency, then invert them so lower cost and lower latency raise the score.</li>
      <li>Combine the normalized values with the user weights.</li>
      <li>Apply evidence-coverage and approximation penalties if the evidence is thin.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Default weight mix</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Metric</th>
          <th scope="col">Default weight</th>
          <th scope="col">What a higher weight favors</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Intelligence</td>
          <td><strong>35%</strong></td>
          <td>General reasoning on the Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Coding</td>
          <td><strong>15%</strong></td>
          <td>Coding benchmarks.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Agentic</td>
          <td><strong>10%</strong></td>
          <td>Tool use and multi-step agent benchmarks.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Task cost</td>
          <td><strong>30%</strong></td>
          <td>Lower dollar cost per task.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Latency</td>
          <td><strong>5%</strong></td>
          <td>Faster first-token and round-trip response.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Throughput</td>
          <td><strong>5%</strong></td>
          <td>More output tokens per second.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Capability per dollar</h3>
    <p>
      The explorer also shows raw <em>capability per dollar</em>.
      That value is the domain score divided by task cost.
      It is <strong>not</strong> the Efficiency Score.
      Near-zero prices cannot hide other ranking factors in silence.
    </p>

    <h2>Usage profiles</h2>
    <p>
      A <strong>usage profile</strong> changes the domain evidence mix, the default weights, and the token workload for estimated task cost.
      The explorer includes eight profiles.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Profile</th>
          <th scope="col">Focus</th>
          <th scope="col">Workload label</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>General</td>
          <td>Broad capability with balanced cost.</td>
          <td>Standard mixed task</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Coding</td>
          <td>Software engineering strength and agent loops.</td>
          <td>Coding agent loop</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Agents</td>
          <td>Tool use, multi-step reliability, and cache.</td>
          <td>Multi-step agent</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Math and Science</td>
          <td>Hard reasoning and science questions.</td>
          <td>Hard reasoning</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Finance</td>
          <td>Numeric reasoning and document extraction.</td>
          <td>Document analysis</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Research</td>
          <td>Long context, retrieval, and synthesis.</td>
          <td>Long-context research</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Writing and Literature</td>
          <td>Style, instruction following, and long output.</td>
          <td>Long-form writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Multimodal</td>
          <td>Image, audio, or video plus text.</td>
          <td>Vision + text</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Approximations</h2>
    <p>
      Many providers publish only some effort levels.
      Missing reasoning variants use this ladder:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Exact measured variant.</strong> Direct measured evidence for that effort.</li>
      <li><strong>Same-family interpolation.</strong> Estimate between two measured efforts in the same family.</li>
      <li><strong>Nearest-effort extrapolation.</strong> Estimate from the closest measured effort.</li>
      <li><strong>Family aggregate plus effort curve.</strong> Estimate from the family pattern.</li>
      <li><strong>Insufficient data.</strong> No fabricated value.</li>
    </ol>

    <blockquote>
      <p>
        <strong>Measured</strong> and <strong>estimated</strong> metrics stay labeled in the UI.
        If confidence is limited, conservative ranking uses the less favorable bound of an estimate.
      </p>
    </blockquote>

    <h2>Data freshness</h2>
    <p>
      A GitHub Actions workflow refreshes the matrix daily at <strong>04:00 UTC</strong>.
      The health endpoint reports matrix age and sync status.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Freshness: <a href="/api/health"><code>/api/health</code></a></li>
      <li>Matrix JSON: <a href="/api/models.json"><code>/api/models.json</code></a></li>
      <li>Matrix CSV: <a href="/api/models.csv"><code>/api/models.csv</code></a></li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      <a href="/">Open the model explorer</a>
      to change floors, weights, and profiles against the live matrix.
    </p>
  </main>
</Layout>
